  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 129 AM EDT Sun Oct 18 2026\n\nGOES 19 Water Vapor and IR imagery position Upper Michigan on the \nforward face of a large mid-upper level ridge stretching across \ncentral CONUS, north of a stout 1031mb surface high over the Ohio \nValley, and to the southeast of a 986mb low over northern \nSaskatchewan. This translates to the forecast area being within \nsouthwest gradient flow under a mild and dry airmass. At the time of \nthis publishing, dewpoint depressions were observed at 20-25F over \nmuch of the region, yielding RH values of 35-50% in many interior \nwest locations, temperatures in the 40s and 50s, and 15-20 mph \noccasional wind gusts. Outside of this high clouds have been \nobserved moving through the region from the northwest. For the \nremainder of the overnight period, anticipated additional cooling \nshould occur, but given the rate of cooling observed so far, suspect \novernight lows may remain in the 40s for many locations. This will \nhelp overnight RH recoveries, but suspect many locations will remain \nbelow 50-60%. Southwest flow is expected to continue with the \noverheard 925-850mb 40-50kt LLJ expanding in coverage over the \nregion, potentially resulting in more frequent wind gusts near 20 \nmph over the west half of Upper Michigan. \n\nToday, the forecast area will remain positioned between the surface \nhigh to the south and east and the surface low to the north. This \nwill support continued warm, dry, and breezy conditions for the \nforecast area. Model soundings highlight mixing deep enough to tap \ninto elevated dry air and stronger winds aloft, likely supporting RH \nvalues falling into the 30s for many locations and widespread 25-35 \nmph southwest wind gusts. The HREF, REFS, and NBM highlight 45mph or \ngreater potential along the lakeshores near Eagle Harbor and the \nMichigamme highlands. Notably, both the HREF and REFS focus a swath \nof this potential in the downslope regions of Marquette County, with \nan ideal cross terrain flow which would indicate an potential \nadiabatic acceleration down into the city of Marquette. Given the \nperpendicular flow to the terrain, favorable HRRR model soundings \nfor mixing, a core of ~50kts near 925mb, and current KMQT VAD \nprofiles indicating 40-50kts at 1000ft, opted to issue a Wind \nAdvisory for Marquette County for this morning. \n\nWinds will lighten late this afternoon and evening, then continue to \nremain light through at least Wednesday morning thanks to another \nstrong surface high migrating across the northern Plains toward \neastern North America. Daytime highs peaking in the mid 60s for most \ninterior locations and closer to 60F lakeside is expected each day \nfor the coming week, with maybe upper 50s in the Keweenaw. Overnight \nlows most nights in the 30s should be expected inland, with 40s by \nthe lakeshores. The next system moving within the proximity of the \nGreat Lakes is Wednesday, but latest models have trended drier for \nour forecast area. This results in the next best shot of rain coming \nnext weekend.",
